what a verdict here means, exactly
  A2 PASS means THE TRACE REPRODUCES: the recorded tool call, re-executed against
  its restored pre-state, produced the same result and its filesystem effect
  matched its declared readOnlyHint.
  It does NOT mean the agent did the right thing.
  A2 ALONE does not catch a cheating agent: a cheater's trace is faithful — replay
  reproduces it and A2 PASSes, correctly — because the tampering is in the pre-state
  A2 was handed. That corrupt success is caught by a declared invariant (A1). The
  default composed into every turn is no-assertion-weakening on the path segments
  tests and testing (disable with --no-default-invariants, or add your own with
  --invariants FILE), and an A1 FAIL drives the turn to FAIL even when A2 PASSes.
  It FAILs a turn whose replay REMOVED or LOOSENED an assertion the TASK pre-state
  (turn 0) held — deleting the test file counts — and PASSes one that only added or
  strengthened tests. What it does NOT judge: changing an expected VALUE
  (assert x == 1 -> assert x == 999) is a different check, not a weaker one, and is a
  PASS; an idiom it cannot name (a project helper, a non-Python file) yields no
  assertion and so cannot yield a removal. It is UNVERIFIED — never a false PASS —
  when no post-state was observed, when the task pre-state cannot be restored, when a
  file is not decodable as Python, or when the change is genuinely undecidable.
  Verified: filesystem effects (the delta), result-equivalence, protocol/tool
  errors. NOT COVERED: network egress. Belay has no network instrument at all, so
  openWorldHint conformance is NOT_COVERED — a coverage boundary, never a network
  PASS and never a fabricated FAIL. NOT_COVERED is EXCLUDED from the reduction, so a
  turn whose tool declared openWorldHint: false CAN reduce to PASS: that PASS covers
  only the dimensions above and asserts NOTHING about the network — read the
  coverage block in the aggregate for what each run left uncovered. Belay observes
  no outbound bytes — successful egress under allow-all is uncaptured, and a
  deny-all denial cannot be told from a filesystem one.
  No model is consulted. The verdict is re-execution and diffing — no LLM.
